From: corruptmods <13516012+corruptmods@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] player: back off passthrough for a codec that failed on
 this route

Since 0040 a passthrough output that fails while playing (the AVPlayer sink
or ao_spatial) makes the track fall back to PCM, and the fallback belongs to
that track: the next file, and any later output reload, try passthrough
again. When the failure is not transient (a host that never services the
main queue, a route AVPlayer cannot drive), every file pays the ~4.4 s
again before it gets audio.

The player now remembers, per codec, a passthrough failure reported through
AO_EVENT_PASSTHROUGH_FAILED together with the audio route it happened on
(ao_get_route_id(): the AVAudioSession route's output ports on iOS/tvOS,
the default output device's UID on macOS). While the route is the same, a
new decoder for that codec starts as PCM and an output reload does not
retry it. A different route clears the entry and passthrough is tried
again; an unknown route counts as the same. Any device change the route
reports after the failure also clears the whole table, the same device
coming back included, since a receiver that was off or in the wrong mode
often takes passthrough once it returns: on iOS/tvOS the player keeps an
AVAudioSession route-change watch from its first failure until it is
destroyed, counting new/old device, override and route-configuration
changes but not category changes, which a PCM output's own session setup
posts right after the fallback. macOS has no such listener and relies on
the route identity. The table lives in MPContext
(8 codecs, oldest dropped), so it is per player and ends with it; nothing
is process-wide. Failures at output init cost nothing and are not
recorded.

An entry also lapses after --audio-spdif-backoff-expiry seconds (default
600, 0 never), and once passthrough of any codec has played on the same
route for 10 s, so one transient failure cannot cost passthrough for the
session.

--audio-spdif-backoff defaults to no, exactly 0040's behaviour, until a
device shows how tvOS reports route changes around a receiver waking and
around the PCM output's own session setup.

test_audio_passthrough_fallback.sh drives it: the failure, a reload and the
next file on the same route, another codec, an unknown route, a route
change for a decoder and for a reload, a device change on the same route
id (only one after the failure counts), a second player, the expiry, the
lapse after passthrough plays (not while paused, not for PCM, not for
another route), the option off, and the bound; its driver checks the
defaults.


